text:
Maya: heads-up — trying to stream completions into a RN WebView. Inside the page I do `fetch` with an Authorization header, but on iOS WKWebView navigation and reloads keep dropping whatever native header injection I tried. Need a reliable pattern for token rotation + streaming. anyone?

Tom: yea ran into this. WKWebView won't let you set custom headers for the initial HTML load (navigation). But once the page is loaded, fetch/XHR from the page can set headers. the pain is SSE/EventSource — CORS + cookies + long-lived connections cause trouble.

Anya: usual pattern that worked for us: keep streaming connection in native (NSURLSession/OkHttp) and bridge messages into the webview via postMessage. native can attach Authorization easily and handle rotation. for simpler cases, pass an ephemeral token into the webview via postMessage and use fetch with Authorization from JS. short-lived token only.

Maya: thanks. i'm worried about exposing token in postMessage payload. is that ok?

Anya: acceptable if token TTL is < 60s and native rotates it proactively. postMessage is just IPC within the app; treat it like any inter-process comm. don't persist token in localStorage of the page.

Luis: another option: polyfill EventSource with a fetch-stream reader in the page so you control headers. e.g.

```js
// eventsource-like via fetch + ReadableStream
const res = await fetch(url, { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } });
const reader = res.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while (true) {
  const { done, value } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buf += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
  // parse by newlines / SSE frames
}
```

Luis: downside: on iOS WKWebView older versions can drop streaming bodies intermittently. native streaming is more robust for long lived sessions.

Tom: fyi if you try cookies: SameSite and iOS default blocking will bite you. and `fetch(..., { credentials: 'include' })` is only workable when you control CORS and cookie flags (SameSite=None; Secure). but cookies don't help with rotation unless you use short cookie TTL and refresh endpoints.

Maya: what about using query param for ephemeral key? simpler to wire into a plain EventSource url.

Luis: don't put secrets in URL. logs, proxies, and referers leak it. last-resort for some older SSE clients but avoid.

Anya: quick native iOS example we ship in our mobile wrappers:
```swift
// Swift: URLSession streaming delegate -> forward to WebView JS
let task = session.dataTask(with: request)
// implement URLSessionDataDelegate to get incremental data and call webView.evaluateJavaScript("window.onChunk(\"...\")")
task.resume()
```

Anya: Kotlin/Android same idea with OkHttp's newEventSource or streaming `call.enqueue` and pipe to `webView.postMessage`.

Maya: thanks — i'll prototype native proxy + postMessage + ephemeral token rotation.

Tom: ping me if you want help wiring the native side. i can push an example wrapper for both iOS + Android.

deploy-bot: reminded: mobile wrappers have a small sample in `/mobile/sdk/examples/webview-stream-bridge` (internal).

Maya: perfect. i'll try native streaming + avoid query params, keep TTL short, and won't persist on JS side. will report back with perf and reconnect behavior. :thumbsup:
